{:edb/db-before-t 1123 :edb/db-after-t 1124 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534436 50 #inst "2026-10-11T20:44:14.903Z" 13194139534436 17592186045417 true] [17592186119619 1071 "Determine if $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\},$ where\n![](images/3c/3c308ca93d8a6fa7ac5c6a037fa3f0a409d7253513db04922cc622750e230691.svg)" 13194139534436 17592186045417 true] [17592186119619 1071 "Determine if $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\},$ where\n![](images/df/df4f126da2ab3a0661bcb7dc18bb1e5eae8cd48d5c5a1464ea580ccc439195d6.png)" 13194139534436 17592186045417 false] [17592186119671 1071 "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n![](images/39/399d7fed8338969b654454d30f2ee661edbd52b3a61d065f33972be21996e188.png)" 13194139534436 17592186045417 false] [17592186119671 1071 "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n![](images/a5/a5ce805c69da0bd2e941aa56a9781ed430c8f0ce7b646bf2a6ea04168ee10cda.svg)" 13194139534436 17592186045417 true] [17592186119808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421529 13194139534436 17592186045417 true] [17592186119808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421530 13194139534436 17592186045417 true] [17592186119809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421528 13194139534436 17592186045417 true] [17592186119856 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421531 13194139534436 17592186045417 true] [17592186119856 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421532 13194139534436 17592186045417 true] [17592186120046 1050 960.0 13194139534436 17592186045417 true] [17592186121035 1064 "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ the set of all possible linear combinations of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe set $U$ is also called the **linear span** of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are the **generators** of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the linear span generated by the following vectors:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of linear span, we know that if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ then $\\mathbf{b}$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1\\mathbf{a_1} + x_2\\mathbf{a_2}\n$$\n\n for some $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the linear span of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGeometrically, $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ can be viewed as the plane $\\Pi$ that passes through the origin and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5b202475e0bc3ebb07df1f07bab72e9e74b13a99196c2a8a5adb624a7534a03.png)" 13194139534436 17592186045417 false] [17592186121035 1064 "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ the set of all possible linear combinations of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe set $U$ is also called the **linear span** of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are the **generators** of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the linear span generated by the following vectors:\n![](images/55/55c749dccc54c252b9120f148e383f395c09fb4cd4bec0e1cce8ed441ecb6199.svg)\n\nFrom the definition of linear span, we know that if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ then $\\mathbf{b}$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1\\mathbf{a_1} + x_2\\mathbf{a_2}\n$$\n\n for some $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the linear span of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n![](images/21/2181b471f34906eeaea7889abbba37c797a2f9624cee455b43b00fc048247cd1.svg)\n\nGeometrically, $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ can be viewed as the plane $\\Pi$ that passes through the origin and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5b202475e0bc3ebb07df1f07bab72e9e74b13a99196c2a8a5adb624a7534a03.png)" 13194139534436 17592186045417 true] [17592186121036 1064 "The linear span of vectors can be interpreted geometrically as the points in space that can be reached by adding multiples of those vectors.\n\n- The span of a single nonzero vector is a *line* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ determines the *line* with direction vector $\\mathbf{a}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ represents the line $l$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/08/080aa6203494e915e28c491a3d86176f7ba634bcbdfe40b094c07d85fb29bb69.png)\n\n- The span of two non-parallel vectors is a *plane* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\not\\parallel \\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel), then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$ determines the *plane* with direction vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$  represents the plane $\\Pi$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/56/5606535bd91eb35745a9852ceb451720339105f5a97fb66ccfcb1164ecd55ffd.png)" 13194139534436 17592186045417 false] [17592186121036 1064 "The linear span of vectors can be interpreted geometrically as the points in space that can be reached by adding multiples of those vectors.\n\n- The span of a single nonzero vector is a *line* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ determines the *line* with direction vector $\\mathbf{a}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n![](images/dd/ddef437718d3a11811da02a17e2207d75e2d1852eeada67ab494af45b2a655e2.svg)\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ represents the line $l$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/08/080aa6203494e915e28c491a3d86176f7ba634bcbdfe40b094c07d85fb29bb69.png)\n\n- The span of two non-parallel vectors is a *plane* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\not\\parallel \\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel), then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$ determines the *plane* with direction vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n![](images/1f/1f92378ff1d1409f0928caa4b3ce